1
00:00:02,158 --> 00:00:09,359
Day after day, the spider waited head down for an idea to come to her. Hour by

2
00:00:09,359 --> 00:00:14,000
hour, she sat motionless, deep in thought.

3
00:00:14,000 --> 00:00:20,399
Having promised Wilbur that she would save his life, she was determined to keep her promise,

4
00:00:20,399 --> 00:00:24,880
Charlotte was naturally patient.

5
00:00:24,880 --> 00:00:29,840
She knew from experience that if she waited long enough, a fly would come to

6
00:00:29,840 --> 00:00:35,198
her web, and she felt sure that if she thought long enough about Wilbur's

7
00:00:35,198 --> 00:00:39,359
problem, an idea would come to her mind.

8
00:00:39,359 --> 00:00:45,920
Finally, one morning toward the middle of July, the idea came. Why, how

9
00:00:45,920 --> 00:00:49,439
perfectly simple, she said to herself.

10
00:00:49,439 --> 00:00:56,079
The way to save Wilbur's life is to play a trick on Zuckerman. If I can fool a

11
00:00:56,079 --> 00:01:05,439
bug, thought Charlotte, I can surely fool a man. People are not as smart as bugs.

12
00:01:05,439 --> 00:01:10,319
Wilbur walked into his yard just at that moment. "What are you thinking about,

13
00:01:10,319 --> 00:01:16,719
Charlotte?" he asked. "I was just thinking," said the spider. "That people

14
00:01:16,719 --> 00:01:24,719
are very gullible." "What does gullible mean?" "Easy to fool," said Charlotte.

15
00:01:24,719 --> 00:01:30,079
"That's a mercy," replied Wilbur, and he lay down in the shade of his fence and

16
00:01:30,079 --> 00:01:33,600
went fast asleep.

17
00:01:33,600 --> 00:01:39,359
The spider, however, stayed wide awake, gazing affectionately at him and making

18
00:01:39,359 --> 00:01:45,359
plans for his future. Summer was half gone. She knew she didn't have much

19
00:01:45,359 --> 00:01:51,439
time. That morning, just as Wilbur fell asleep, Avery Arable wandered into the

20
00:01:51,439 --> 00:02:00,319
Zuckerman's front yard, followed by Fern. Avery carried a live frog in his hand.

21
00:02:00,319 --> 00:02:03,840
Fern had a crown of daisies in her hair.

22
00:02:03,840 --> 00:02:09,359
The children ran for the kitchen. "Just in time for a piece of blueberry pie,"

23
00:02:09,359 --> 00:02:14,560
said Mrs. Zuckerman. "Look at my frog," said Avery, placing the frog on the

24
00:02:14,560 --> 00:02:20,079
drain board and holding out his hand for pie. "Take that thing out of here," said

25
00:02:20,079 --> 00:02:23,679
Mrs. Zuckerman. "He's hot," said Fern.

26
00:02:23,679 --> 00:02:29,600
"He's almost dead, that frog." "He is not," said Avery. He lets me scratch him

27
00:02:29,600 --> 00:02:38,719
between the eyes. The frog jumped and landed in Mrs. Zuckermanman's dishpan full of soapy water.

28
00:02:38,719 --> 00:02:43,760
You're getting your pow on you, said Fern. Can I look for eggs in the hen

29
00:02:43,760 --> 00:02:50,238
house and Edith? Run outdoors, both of you, and don't bother the hens.

30
00:02:50,238 --> 00:02:55,439
It's getting all over everything, shouted Fern. His pie is all over his

31
00:02:55,439 --> 00:03:02,079
front. Come on, frog! cried Avery. He scooped up his frog. The frog kicked,

32
00:03:02,079 --> 00:03:08,158
splashing soapy water onto the blueberry pie. "Another crisis!" groaned Fern.

33
00:03:08,158 --> 00:03:11,198
"Let's swing in the swing," said Avery.

34
00:03:11,198 --> 00:03:15,279
The children ran to the barn.

35
00:03:15,279 --> 00:03:21,600
Mr. Zuckerman had the best swing in the county. "It was a single long piece of

36
00:03:21,600 --> 00:03:26,639
heavy rope tied to the beam over the north doorway. At the bottom end of the

37
00:03:26,639 --> 00:03:32,158
rope was a fat knot to sit on. It was arranged so that you could swing without

38
00:03:32,158 --> 00:03:38,238
being pushed. You climbed a ladder to the hoft. Then, holding the rope, you

39
00:03:38,238 --> 00:03:43,600
stood at the edge and looked down and were scared and dizzy. Then you

40
00:03:43,600 --> 00:03:48,800
straddled the knot so that it acted as a seat. Then you got up all your nerve,

41
00:03:48,800 --> 00:03:53,359
took a deep breath, and jumped.

42
00:03:53,359 --> 00:03:59,198
For a second you seemed to be falling to the barn floor far below. But then

43
00:03:59,198 --> 00:04:03,760
suddenly the rope would begin to catch you and you would sail through the barn

44
00:04:03,760 --> 00:04:08,719
door going a mile a minute with the wind whistling in your eyes and ears and

45
00:04:08,719 --> 00:04:14,960
hair. Then you would zoom upward into the sky and look up at the clouds and

46
00:04:14,960 --> 00:04:20,960
the rope would twist and you would twist and turn with the rope.

47
00:04:20,960 --> 00:04:26,639
Then you would drop down down down out of the sky and come sailing back into

48
00:04:26,639 --> 00:04:32,800
the barn, almost into the hoft. Then sail out again, not quite so far this

49
00:04:32,800 --> 00:04:36,479
time. Then in again, not quite so high.

50
00:04:36,479 --> 00:04:39,840
Then out again. Then in again, then out.

51
00:04:39,840 --> 00:04:47,198
Then in. And then you jump off and fall down and let somebody else try it.

52
00:04:47,198 --> 00:04:52,158
Mothers for miles around worried about Zuckermanman's swing. They feared some

53
00:04:52,158 --> 00:04:58,560
child would fall off, but no child ever did. Children almost always hang on to

54
00:04:58,560 --> 00:05:03,520
things tighter than their parents think they will. Avery put the frog in his

55
00:05:03,520 --> 00:05:08,238
pocket and climbed to the hoft. "The last time I swang in this swing, I

56
00:05:08,238 --> 00:05:16,479
almost crashed into a barn swallow," he yelled. "Take that frog out!" ordered Fern.

57
00:05:16,479 --> 00:05:22,319
Avery straddled the rope and jumped. He sailed out through the door, frog and

58
00:05:22,319 --> 00:05:26,238
all, and into the sky. Frog and all.

59
00:05:26,238 --> 00:05:33,279
Then he sailed back into the barn. Your tongue is purple, screamed Fern. So is

60
00:05:33,279 --> 00:05:39,198
yours, cried Avery, sailing out again with the frog. I have hay inside my

61
00:05:39,198 --> 00:05:46,560
dress. It itches, called Fern. Scratch it, yelled Avery as he sailed back. It's

62
00:05:46,560 --> 00:05:55,840
my turn, said Fern. Jump off. Fern's got the itch, sang Avery.

63
00:05:55,840 --> 00:06:00,800
When he jumped off, he threw the swing up to his sister. She shut her eyes

64
00:06:00,800 --> 00:06:06,800
tight and jumped. She felt the dizzy drop, then the supporting lift of the

65
00:06:06,800 --> 00:06:12,399
swing. When she opened her eyes, she was looking up into the blue sky and was

66
00:06:12,399 --> 00:06:19,359
about to fly back through the door. They took turns for an hour.

67
00:06:19,359 --> 00:06:23,198
When the children grew tired of swinging, they went down toward the

68
00:06:23,198 --> 00:06:28,079
pasture and picked wild raspberries and ate them. Their tongues turned from

69
00:06:28,079 --> 00:06:34,399
purple to red. Fern bit into a raspberry that had a bad tasting bug inside it and

70
00:06:34,399 --> 00:06:40,399
got discouraged. Avery found an empty candy box and put his frog in it. The

71
00:06:40,399 --> 00:06:45,600
frog seemed tired after his morning in the swing. The children walked slowly up

72
00:06:45,600 --> 00:06:52,960
toward the barn. They too were tired and hardly had energy enough to walk.

73
00:06:52,960 --> 00:06:58,079
"Let's build a treehouse," suggested Avery. "I want to live in a tree with my

74
00:06:58,079 --> 00:07:04,079
frog." "I'm going to visit Wilbur," Fern announced. They climbed the fence into

75
00:07:04,079 --> 00:07:09,198
the lane and walked lazily toward the pig pen. Wilbur heard them coming and

76
00:07:09,198 --> 00:07:16,639
got up. Avery noticed the spiderweb and coming closer he saw Charlotte. "Hey,

77
00:07:16,639 --> 00:07:22,960
look at that big spider," he said. "It's tremendous." "Leave it alone," commanded

78
00:07:22,960 --> 00:07:28,639
Fern. "You've got a frog. Isn't that enough?"

79
00:07:28,639 --> 00:07:33,439
That's a fine spider, and I'm going to capture it," said Avery. He took the

80
00:07:33,439 --> 00:07:38,319
cover off the candy box. Then he picked up a stick. "I'm going to knock that old

81
00:07:38,319 --> 00:07:44,319
spider into this box," he said. Wilbur's heart almost stopped when he saw what

82
00:07:44,319 --> 00:07:49,118
was going on. "This might be the end of Charlotte if the boy succeeded in

83
00:07:49,118 --> 00:07:52,639
catching her." "You stop it, Avery!"

84
00:07:52,639 --> 00:07:55,600
cried Fern.

85
00:07:55,600 --> 00:08:00,880
Avery put one leg over the fence of the pig pin. He was just about to raise his

86
00:08:00,880 --> 00:08:06,479
stick to hit Charlotte when he lost his balance. He swayed and toppled and

87
00:08:06,479 --> 00:08:09,198
landed on the edge of Wilbur's trough.

88
00:08:09,198 --> 00:08:15,840
The trough tipped up and then came down with a slap. The goose egg was right

89
00:08:15,840 --> 00:08:26,560
underneath. There was a dull explosion as the egg broke and then a horrible smell.

90
00:08:26,560 --> 00:08:30,560
Fern screamed. Avery jumped to his feet.

91
00:08:30,560 --> 00:08:36,000
The air was filled with the terrible gases and smells from the rotten egg.

92
00:08:36,000 --> 00:08:41,840
Templeton, who had been resting in his home, scuttled away into the barn. "Good

93
00:08:41,840 --> 00:08:45,359
night!" screamed Avery. "Good night!

94
00:08:45,359 --> 00:08:48,560
What a stink! Let's get out of here!"

95
00:08:48,560 --> 00:08:54,479
Fern was crying. She held her nose and ran toward the house. Avery ran after

96
00:08:54,479 --> 00:09:00,960
her, holding his nose. Charlotte felt greatly relieved to see him go. It had

97
00:09:00,960 --> 00:09:04,639
been a narrow escape.

98
00:09:04,639 --> 00:09:09,520
Later on that morning, the animals came up from the pasture, the sheep, the

99
00:09:09,520 --> 00:09:14,639
lambs, the gander, the goose, and the seven gosslings. There were many

100
00:09:14,639 --> 00:09:19,760
complaints about the awful smell, and Wilbur had to tell the story over and

101
00:09:19,760 --> 00:09:24,880
over again of how the arable boy had tried to capture Charlotte, and how the

102
00:09:24,880 --> 00:09:30,639
smell of the broken egg drove him away just in time. "It was that rotten goose

103
00:09:30,639 --> 00:09:35,679
egg that saved Charlotte's life," said Wilbur.

104
00:09:35,679 --> 00:09:41,359
The goose was proud of her share in the adventure. Oh, I'm delighted that the

105
00:09:41,359 --> 00:09:44,399
egg never hatched," she gabbled.

106
00:09:44,399 --> 00:09:50,319
Templeton, of course, was miserable over the loss of his beloved egg, but he

107
00:09:50,319 --> 00:09:57,039
couldn't resist boasting. "It pays to save things," he said in his surirly

108
00:09:57,039 --> 00:10:04,238
voice. "A rat never knows when something is going to come in handy. I never throw

109
00:10:04,238 --> 00:10:07,439
anything away."

110
00:10:07,439 --> 00:10:13,198
Well, said one of the lambs, this whole business is all well and good for

111
00:10:13,198 --> 00:10:20,238
Charlotte, but what about the rest of us? The smell is unbearable.

112
00:10:20,238 --> 00:10:26,238
Who wants to live in a barn that is perfumed with rotten egg? "Don't worry,

113
00:10:26,238 --> 00:10:29,039
you'll get used to it," said Templeton.

114
00:10:29,039 --> 00:10:35,439
He sat up and pulled wisely at his long whiskers, then crept away to pay a visit

115
00:10:35,439 --> 00:10:38,238
to the dump.

116
00:10:38,238 --> 00:10:43,359
When Lurvy showed up at lunchtime carrying a pale of food for Wilbur, he

117
00:10:43,359 --> 00:10:49,840
stopped short a few paces from the pig pin. He sniffed the air and made a face.

118
00:10:49,840 --> 00:10:55,679
"What in thunder?" he said, setting the pale down. He picked up the stick that

119
00:10:55,679 --> 00:11:05,198
Avery had dropped and pried the trough up. "Rats," he said. "Ph, I might have known a rat

120
00:11:05,198 --> 00:11:12,399
would make a nest under this trough. How I hate a rat."

121
00:11:12,399 --> 00:11:17,439
And Lurvy dragged Wilbur's trough across the yard and kicked some dirt into the

122
00:11:17,439 --> 00:11:24,000
rat's nest, burying the broken egg and all Templeton's other possessions.

123
00:11:24,000 --> 00:11:29,840
Then he picked up the pale. Wilbur stood in the trough, drooling with hunger.

124
00:11:29,840 --> 00:11:36,479
Lurvy poured. The slops ran creamily down around the pig's eyes and ears.

125
00:11:36,479 --> 00:11:43,279
Wilbur grunted. He gulped and sucked and sucked and gulped, making swishing and

126
00:11:43,279 --> 00:11:49,198
swishing noises, anxious to get everything at once.

127
00:11:49,198 --> 00:11:56,158
It was a delicious meal. Skim milk, wheat midlings, leftover pancakes, half

128
00:11:56,158 --> 00:12:02,158
a donut, the rind of a summer squash, two pieces of stale toast, a third of a

129
00:12:02,158 --> 00:12:07,679
ginger snap, a fishtail, one orange peel, several noodles from a noodle

130
00:12:07,679 --> 00:12:13,600
soup, the scum off a cup of cocoa, an ancient jelly roll, a strip of paper

131
00:12:13,600 --> 00:12:20,880
from the lining of the garbage pail, and a spoonful of raspberry Jell-O.

132
00:12:20,880 --> 00:12:26,479
Wilbur ate heartily. He planned to leave half a noodle and a few drops of milk

133
00:12:26,479 --> 00:12:31,279
for Templeton. Then he remembered that the rat had been useful in saving

134
00:12:31,279 --> 00:12:37,198
Charlotte's life and that Charlotte was trying to save his life. So he left a

135
00:12:37,198 --> 00:12:41,359
whole noodle instead of a half.

136
00:12:41,359 --> 00:12:46,079
Now that the broken egg was buried, the air cleared and the barn smelled good

137
00:12:46,079 --> 00:12:53,920
again. The afternoon passed and evening came. Shadows lengthened. The cool and

138
00:12:53,920 --> 00:12:58,560
kindly breath of evening entered through the doors and windows.

139
00:12:58,560 --> 00:13:04,479
A stride her web, Charlotte sat moodily, eating a horsefly and thinking about the

140
00:13:04,479 --> 00:13:10,719
future. After a while she burred herself.

141
00:13:10,719 --> 00:13:15,359
She descended to the center of the web, and there she began to cut some of her

142
00:13:15,359 --> 00:13:21,039
lines. She worked slowly but steadily while the other creatures drowsed. None

143
00:13:21,039 --> 00:13:26,158
of the others, not even the goose, noticed that she was at work. Deep in

144
00:13:26,158 --> 00:13:36,000
his soft bed, Wilbur snoozed. Over in their favorite corner, the golings whistled a night song.

145
00:13:36,000 --> 00:13:40,719
Charlotte tore quite a section out of her web, leaving an open space in the

146
00:13:40,719 --> 00:13:45,118
middle. Then she started weaving something to take the place of the

147
00:13:45,118 --> 00:13:50,719
threads she had removed. When Templeton got back from the dump around midnight,

148
00:13:50,719 --> 00:13:55,479
the spider was still at work.

149
00:13:56,319 --> 00:13:58,800
Chapter 11.

150
00:13:58,800 --> 00:14:01,000
The miracle.
